window.OPS_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"The Linux Command Line","f":"p1-linux-command-line.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You run sudo rm /var/log/app/debug.log (13 GB), but df -h still shows the disk 100% full. Why?","o":["df caches its numbers for a few minutes","A process still has the file open, so its blocks are freed only when that process closes it","rm only moves files to a trash folder","You need to run sync first"],"a":1,"w":"rm removes the directory entry. The data stays allocated while any process holds an open descriptor. lsof +L1 finds it; restart the process or truncate the file instead."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"The Linux Command Line","f":"p1-linux-command-line.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"nginx logs \"connect() failed (111: Connection refused) while connecting to upstream 127.0.0.1:8080\". Which command checks the most likely cause?","o":["df -h","chmod 777 /etc/nginx","apt-get install nginx","sudo ss -ltnp , to see whether anything is listening on port 8080"],"a":3,"w":"\"Connection refused\" means no process is listening on that address and port. ss shows listening sockets and, with sudo, which process owns each."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"The Linux Command Line","f":"p1-linux-command-line.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does chmod 640 config.yaml allow?","o":["The owner reads and writes, the group reads, everyone else has no access","Everyone reads, only the owner writes","The owner reads, the group writes, others execute","Nobody can read it except root"],"a":0,"w":"Each digit is a sum of r = 4, w = 2, x = 1, for owner, group and others in that order: 6 = rw-, 4 = r--, 0 = ---."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"The Linux Command Line","f":"p1-linux-command-line.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the difference between kill 4242 and kill -9 4242 ?","o":["There is none; -9 is just faster","The first kills the process, the second restarts it","The first sends SIGTERM, which the process can catch to clean up; -9 sends SIGKILL, which ends it immediately with no cleanup","-9 kills all processes with that name"],"a":2,"w":"SIGTERM (15) is a request; well-behaved services finish in-flight work and exit. SIGKILL (9) cannot be caught or ignored, so nothing gets cleaned up."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"The Linux Command Line","f":"p1-linux-command-line.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which pipeline correctly lists the five IPs (first field) with the most requests in access.log ?","o":["awk '{print $1}' access.log | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -5","awk '{print $1}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -5","grep -c '$1' access.log | head -5","sort access.log | head -5"],"a":1,"w":"uniq -c only counts adjacent identical lines, so the input must be sorted first. Without the first sort, one IP appears many times with small counts."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Bash Scripting","f":"p2-bash-scripting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A script has set -e but not pipefail . What happens on pg_dump shop | gzip > backup.gz when pg_dump fails?","o":["The script stops, because set -e sees pg_dump fail","Bash prints a warning and retries","The script continues: the pipeline's exit code is gzip's, which is 0","gzip fails too, so the script stops"],"a":2,"w":"Without pipefail, a pipeline returns the exit code of its last command. gzip compresses empty input successfully, so the line \"succeeds\" and set -e never fires."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Bash Scripting","f":"p2-bash-scripting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which line deletes the right thing when f=\"old logs.txt\" ?","o":["rm \"$f\"","rm $f","rm '$f'","rm ${f}"],"a":0,"w":"Double quotes keep the value as one argument. Unquoted (with or without braces) it splits into \"old\" and \"logs.txt\"; single quotes pass the literal text $f."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Bash Scripting","f":"p2-bash-scripting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With set -e on, which failing command does not stop the script?","o":["make build on its own line","cp a b on its own line","false on its own line","grep -q ERROR app.log || echo clean"],"a":3,"w":"A command on the left of || (or &&, or in an if/while condition) is being tested, so Bash assumes you handle its failure and carries on."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Bash Scripting","f":"p2-bash-scripting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does \"${DB_URL:?DB_URL must be set}\" do when DB_URL is empty?","o":["Uses the text \"DB_URL must be set\" as the value","Prints the message to stderr and exits the script with an error","Prompts the user to type a value","Nothing; it expands to an empty string"],"a":1,"w":":? is the \"required\" expansion: unset or empty makes a non-interactive shell print the message and exit. :- would supply a default instead."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Bash Scripting","f":"p2-bash-scripting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A while read -r host loop over 40 hosts runs ssh \"$host\" uptime but only the first host is contacted. Why?","o":["ssh exits the script with set -e","read only reads one line by default","ssh reads standard input, which is the rest of the host list; use ssh -n or redirect from /dev/null","The hosts file must be sorted"],"a":2,"w":"Inside the loop, stdin is hosts.txt. ssh consumes it, so the next read finds end of file. ssh -n (or </dev/null) stops that."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Git Workflows","f":"p3-git-workflows.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You rebase your feature branch onto the latest main . What happens to your branch's commit SHAs?","o":["They stay the same; only the branch pointer moves","They change, because each replayed commit has a new parent and the SHA hashes the parent too","They are merged into one SHA","Only the last commit gets a new SHA"],"a":1,"w":"A commit's ID covers its content and its parent. Rebase creates new commits with new parents, so every replayed commit gets a new SHA."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Git Workflows","f":"p3-git-workflows.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"When does git merge feature on main produce a fast-forward rather than a merge commit?","o":["When main has no commits that feature lacks: main's tip is an ancestor of feature's tip","When feature has only one commit","When there are no conflicts","Always, unless you pass --no-ff"],"a":0,"w":"If main has not moved since the branch was created, Git can just move the main pointer forward. If both moved, a merge commit is needed (no conflicts required either way)."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Git Workflows","f":"p3-git-workflows.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A bad commit reached main and later commits followed it. What is the safe way to undo it in Git?","o":["git reset --hard HEAD~4 and force-push","Delete the main branch and recreate it","Rebase main interactively and drop the commit","git revert <sha> , which adds a commit reversing only that change"],"a":3,"w":"Revert adds history instead of rewriting it, so later commits stay and nobody's clone breaks. Reset and rebase rewrite shared history."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Git Workflows","f":"p3-git-workflows.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which situation fits trunk-based development best?","o":["A desktop app released twice a year with long stabilisation periods","A team that wants each feature isolated for weeks","A web service deployed many times a day, with unfinished work behind feature flags","A project with no automated tests"],"a":2,"w":"Trunk-based relies on small, frequent merges, automated tests and feature flags, which is what continuously deployed services have. Scheduled versioned releases suit GitFlow better."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Git Workflows","f":"p3-git-workflows.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why should a published release tag such as v2.4.1 never be moved to another commit?","o":["Git forbids it","Consumers expect the tag to mean the same code forever; moving it silently changes what they build and run","Tags use more disk space when moved","It deletes the commit the tag pointed to"],"a":1,"w":"Git allows it with force, which is the danger: tools that fetch by tag (actions, modules, charts) get different code without anyone noticing. That is how the 2025 tj-actions compromise spread."}];
